==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|biker|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|non|en}} {{term|bikarr||cup|lang=non}}, from {{etyl|osx|en}} {{term|bikeri||cup|lang=osx}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{term/t|la|bīcārium||wine vat, jug}}, of disputed origin. Possibly from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|βίκος||tr=bíkos|earthenware jug, wine jar|lang=grc}}, or from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|bacarium||wine vat, vase|lang=la}}. Cognate with {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|beker||beaker, cup|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Becher||beaker, cup, goblet|lang=de}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|bæger||beaker|lang=da}}, Italian {{term|bicchiere||cup, glass (for drink)|lang=it}}. See also {{l/en|pitcher}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈbiːkə(ɹ)/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈbiːkɚ/}}
* {{rhymes|iːkə(r)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A flat-bottomed [[vessel]], with a [[lip]], used as a [[laboratory]] [[container]].
# A [[drinking]] vessel without a [[handle]], sometimes for the use of [[children]].
# A [[mug]].

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|drinking vessel without a handle}} [[glass]] (2nd definition)

